# Values that differ per deployment. Copy this file to `dsh-pocket.properties` and fill in your own.
#
# `dsh-pocket.properties` is gitignored, so nothing here reaches the repository. Every key is
# optional: leave one out and the build falls back to a neutral default that keeps the app
# buildable, just without that feature.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# applicationId — the app's identity on the device
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This is also the identity a Firebase Android app is registered under. If you already have a
# published build or a working FCM setup, keep using the same value here: changing it produces an
# app that installs alongside the old one and receives no push.
#
# Default when absent: app.dshpocket
# applicationId=com.example.dshpocket

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# appLinkHost — the host that App Links verification runs against
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The pairing QR code is an https link to your APK download with the pairing code in the fragment.
# With this set to a host you control, Android verifies it against
# https://<appLinkHost>/.well-known/assetlinks.json and hands the link to the app instead of the
# browser, which is what makes one scan install and pair.
#
# That file must list this app's applicationId and the SHA-256 fingerprint of the certificate it is
# signed with. See the README for how to produce it.
#
# Default when absent: harness.example.com (a placeholder that never verifies)
# appLinkHost=harness.example.com

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Firebase client configuration — optional, enables push notifications
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# These four are client values that ship inside every Firebase Android app; they are not secrets.
# The actual secret is the service account JSON, which stays on the harness host and never enters
# this project. Leave all four empty to build without push.
#
# firebaseProjectId=your-project-id
# firebaseAppId=1:000000000000:android:0000000000000000000000
# firebaseApiKey=AIza...
# firebaseSenderId=000000000000

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Baked-in pairing — optional
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Set these and a freshly installed app pairs itself with nothing to type or scan. `pairKey` is a
# single-use enrollment code; the harness spends it on first redemption, so a publicly downloadable
# APK cannot be reused by a second person.
#
# pairBase=https://harness.example.com
# pairKey=abcdefgh
